.TH PBUSD 8 "2026-10-25" "pbus @VERSION@" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
pbusd \- pbus event dispatcher daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pbusd
.RB [ \-\-foreground ]
.RB [ \-\-log\-level
.IR level ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pbusd
reads events from the named pipe
.IR /run/pbus/bus ,
checks the key presented, then asynchronously launches the commands registered
in
.IR /etc/pbus/listeners.d .
It is normally started at boot by the
.B pbus.service
unit.
.PP
Event and listener declarations are re-read on every incoming event, so an
edit made by hand applies immediately.
.B SIGHUP
re-reads
.I /etc/pbus/pbus.conf
only.
.SH PROTOCOL
An emission is a single line, fields separated by tabs:
.PP
.RS
PBUS2 <event> <key> <nonce> <timestamp> <reply wanted> <base64 payload>
.RE
.PP
Writes under 4096 bytes to a pipe are atomic, which makes concurrent emissions
safe. Beyond that, the emitter drops the payload into
.I /run/pbus/spool
and sends only the
.B @spool
marker.
.PP
When the sixth field is 1, the daemon drops its verdict into
.IR /run/pbus/reply/<nonce> ,
written under a temporary name and renamed, so the emitter never reads half a
status. The verdict is written before the listeners are launched. Stale
replies are swept after five minutes, as abandoned spool files are after an
hour.
.SH SECURITY
The pipe is created mode
.BR 0622 :
any user may emit, nobody but the daemon may read the traffic. The key
therefore travels in the clear through a channel only the daemon reads; on
disk, only a salted SHA\-256 hash is kept, mode
.BR 0600 .
Comparison is constant-time.
.PP
A listener must set
.IR user .
The daemon refuses to run one whose file does not, logging
.RB \(lq "no user= set: refusing to run" \(rq :
an absent
.I user
used to mean the daemon's own identity, which under the service unit is root,
so the dangerous choice was also the silent one.
.I user=root
reproduces that behaviour explicitly. Privileges are dropped with
.BR setpriv (1),
which is skipped when the named account is already the one the daemon runs as.
A listener with
.I user=root
is as dangerous as a line in root's crontab: grant write access to
.I /etc/pbus/listeners.d
to administrators only.
.PP
An event may declare
.I payload_pattern
in its
.I .event
file, an extended regular expression the payload must match. It is checked
after authentication and before any listener is selected, so a hostile payload
is refused however the listeners are written; the emitter is told, and
.B pbus emit
exits 6. The payload itself is never written to the log. An expression the
shell cannot compile is a configuration fault and refuses every emission for
that event.
.PP
A payload too large for one atomic write to the pipe is left in
.I /run/pbus/spool
by its emitter, in a directory everyone may write to. The daemon does not read
it there: a test on a path and a read of the same path are two operations, and
between them the file's owner may replace it with a symbolic link that the
daemon would follow as root. It is moved first into
.IR /run/pbus/stage ,
mode
.BR 0700 ,
with
.BR rename (2),
which acts on the name itself and is atomic; once there, nothing outside the
daemon can change it, and a symbolic link, a link count above one, or a size
beyond
.I max_payload
is refused or trimmed with a line in the log.
.BR proc (5)
settings
.I fs.protected_symlinks
and
.I fs.protected_hardlinks
guard the same ground and are enabled by default on Debian, but the daemon no
longer depends on them.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.TP
.B log_level
debug, info, warn or error.
.TP
.B log_file
Path of the log.
.TP
.B max_jobs
Maximum number of concurrent commands; beyond it, the daemon waits.
.TP
.B max_payload
Largest payload read, in bytes, default 1048576. Beyond it the payload is
trimmed and the log says so. Above 100000 bytes a payload is also left out of
the triggered command's environment and reaches it on standard input only; see
.I PBUS_PAYLOAD_SIZE
in
.BR pbus (1).
.TP
.B default_timeout
Default maximum run time of a command, in seconds.
.SH FILES
.I /run/pbus/bus
,
.I /run/pbus/spool
,
.I /run/pbus/reply
,
.I /run/pbus/pbusd.pid
,
.I /run/pbus/stats
,
.I /var/log/pbus/pbus.log
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pbus (1),
.BR setpriv (1),
.BR systemd.service (5)
